mod('datastats','Data, graphs & statistics',
 'Two bars of different heights do not prove two groups differ. How spread out the data are, and how many measurements there are, decide that.',
 ['A <b>mean</b> summarizes a sample, but samples vary. The <b>standard deviation</b> (SD) says how far individual measurements typically fall from the mean. The <b>standard error of the mean</b> (SEM = SD/√n) says how precisely the mean itself is known, and it shrinks as you take more measurements.',
  'Error bars are a way to see this. If you plot the mean ± 2 SEM (about a 95% confidence interval) for two groups and the bars <b>do not overlap</b>, the difference is probably real. If they overlap a lot, the data do not support a difference. Always say which kind of error bar a graph uses.'],
 ('a bigger bar meant a bigger effect','how much the data scatter, or how many trials were run','a mean is only an estimate and carries uncertainty','SD, SEM and error bars to decide whether a difference is meaningful'),
 [('mean = Σx / n','average of the sample'),('SD = √[ Σ(x − mean)² / (n − 1) ]','spread of individual values'),('SEM = SD / √n','precision of the mean'),('95% CI ≈ mean ± 2 SEM','used for error bars')],
 [lab('ds-lab','Two groups with error bars',canvas='ds-canvas',aria='Bar graph of two group means with error bars. Controls change the means, spread and sample size.',
   seg=('ds-kind','Error bars show',[('sd','SD'),('sem','SEM'),('ci','2 × SEM (≈95% CI)')]),
   ctl=[('ds-ma','Mean of group A',10,40,1,20),('ds-mb','Mean of group B',10,40,1,26),('ds-sd','SD (both groups)',1,12,.5,6),('ds-n','Sample size n (both)',3,100,1,10)],
   out=[('ds-sem','SEM','x'),('ds-diff','difference in means','v'),('ds-ov','2 × SEM bars','')],own=True)],
 [('Leave the means at 20 and 26 with SD 6 and n 10. Do the 2 × SEM bars overlap? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#ds-kind [data-k=ci]"],["#ds-sd",6],["#ds-n",10]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','They are separated: SEM = 6/√10 ≈ 1.9, so each bar is ±3.8, and 20 + 3.8 < 26 − 3.8. The difference is likely real.'),
  ('Raise the SD to 12 without changing anything else. <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#ds-sd",12]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','The bars now overlap heavily. More scatter means less confidence that the means differ, even though the means are the same.'),
  ('With SD 12, raise n to 100. <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#ds-sd",12],["#ds-n",100]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','SEM falls to 1.2, so the bars separate again. More measurements give a more precise mean.')],
 ('Plant heights (cm) after two treatments: A = 12, 14, 15, 11, 13; B = 17, 19, 16, 18, 20. Find the mean and SD of A and decide whether the groups likely differ.',[
  '<b>Mean of A.</b> (12+14+15+11+13)/5 = 13.',
  '<b>Deviations squared.</b> (−1)², 1², 2², (−2)², 0² = 1, 1, 4, 4, 0, sum 10.',
  '<b>SD.</b> √(10/(5−1)) = √2.5 ≈ 1.58. SEM = 1.58/√5 ≈ 0.71.',
  '<b>Compare.</b> B has mean 18 and the same SD, so 2 × SEM is about 1.4 for each group. 13 + 1.4 is far below 18 − 1.4, so the bars do not overlap: the treatments likely differ.']),
 ['Using n instead of n − 1 for a sample SD.','Confusing SD with SEM. SD describes the data; SEM describes the mean.','Saying overlapping error bars prove no difference. They mean the data do not show one.','Comparing bar heights without looking at the error bars or the sample size.','Forgetting to label the axes with units, or to say what the error bars represent.'],
 ('What a graph must show',['<b>Independent variable on the x-axis, dependent on the y-axis</b>, both labeled with units, with an even scale. Use a line graph for continuous x (temperature, time), a bar graph for categories. For each point or bar, plot a mean and error bars when there are replicates. In an FRQ, describe the trend with numbers ("rate doubled from 20 to 30 °C"), then explain the biology.']),
 [('Two group means have error bars of ±2 SEM that do not overlap. The best conclusion is',['the difference is likely real','the difference is certainly caused by the treatment','the sample size must be too small','the SDs must be equal'],0,'Non-overlapping 2 × SEM bars suggest the means differ, though design and controls still decide about cause.')])

mod('chisq','Chi-square test',
 'Flip a coin 100 times and you will not get exactly 50 heads. The chi-square test asks whether a miss is small enough to be luck.',
 ['Genetics predicts ratios, such as 3:1. Real counts never match exactly, so we need a rule for when the miss is too big. The <b>chi-square</b> statistic adds up how far each observed count is from the expected count, scaled by the expected count: χ² = Σ (o − e)² / e.',
  'Compare χ² with a <b>critical value</b> that depends on the <b>degrees of freedom</b> (df = number of categories − 1) and a significance level, usually p = 0.05. If χ² is <b>less</b> than the critical value, you <b>fail to reject</b> the null hypothesis: the data are consistent with the expected ratio and chance can explain the difference. If χ² is <b>greater</b>, you reject it: the difference is probably not just chance.'],
 ('observed counts that differed from the prediction looked like a failed prediction','that sampling error alone makes counts deviate','a decision rule for "close enough"','chi-square, degrees of freedom and the critical value'),
 [('χ² = Σ (o − e)² / e','sum over every category'),('df = categories − 1','for a ratio test'),('critical value (p = 0.05): df 1: 3.84, df 2: 5.99, df 3: 7.81','from the table'),('χ² < critical → fail to reject the null','difference can be due to chance')],
 [lab('cs-lab','Does this cross fit 3 : 1?',canvas='cs-canvas',aria='Bars comparing observed and expected counts for a 3 to 1 ratio, with the chi-square result.',
   ctl=[('cs-n','Total offspring',40,400,4,200),('cs-o','Observed dominant phenotype',0,400,1,162)],
   out=[('cs-chi','χ²','x'),('cs-df','degrees of freedom','v'),('cs-crit','critical value, p = 0.05','a'),('cs-dec','decision','')],own=True)],
 [('Total 200, observed dominant 162 (expected 150). Is that consistent with 3 : 1? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#cs-n",200],["#cs-o",162]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','χ² = 12²/150 + 12²/50 = 0.96 + 2.88 = 3.84, right at the critical value of 3.84: that is the boundary, so it just reaches significance. Try 160 to see a clear fail-to-reject.'),
  ('Keep the ratio but double the total to 400 with 324 dominant. <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#cs-n",400],["#cs-o",324]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','The same percentage miss now gives χ² = 7.68, well past 3.84. Bigger samples make the same proportional deviation more convincing.')],
 ('A cross is expected to give 9 : 3 : 3 : 1. Among 160 offspring you count 94, 28, 26, 12. Test the fit.',[
  '<b>Expected counts.</b> 160 × 9/16 = 90, 160 × 3/16 = 30, 30, and 160/16 = 10.',
  '<b>Each term (o − e)²/e.</b> 16/90 = 0.18; 4/30 = 0.13; 16/30 = 0.53; 4/10 = 0.40.',
  '<b>Add.</b> χ² ≈ 1.24 with df = 4 − 1 = 3.',
  '<b>Decide.</b> The critical value for df 3 at p = 0.05 is 7.81. Since 1.24 &lt; 7.81 you fail to reject the null: the data fit 9:3:3:1.']),
 ['Using percentages or proportions instead of counts in the formula.','Computing expected from the observed totals incorrectly (expected must come from the ratio and the same total).','Using df = number of categories instead of categories − 1.','Saying "accept the null". You can only fail to reject it.','Thinking a small χ² proves the hypothesis. It means chance is a sufficient explanation.'],
 ('Writing the conclusion',['State the null hypothesis ("the observed ratio fits 3:1, deviations are due to chance"), give χ² and df, compare with the critical value, and conclude. "χ² = 3.1 &lt; 3.84, so I fail to reject the null; the data are consistent with 3:1."']),
 [('For a cross with 4 phenotype classes, the degrees of freedom are',['3','4','1','2'],0,'df = number of categories − 1 = 3.')])
